\import LambdaBox
\import Data.Bool
\import Data.Maybe
\import Data.String

-- =============================================================================
-- `axiom-mangle-collision`: the same non-injective mangling as
-- ExampleMangleCollision.ard, but reaching a SILENT WRONG ANSWER instead of a
-- build failure -- the class of bug notes/INTERNSHIP_TODOS.md singles out as
-- the valuable one.
--
-- `compileDecl` (ToJava.ard:355) resolves a bodiless constant with
-- `lookupAxiom name javaAxioms`, where `name` is the ALREADY-MANGLED kername
-- and the table's keys (ToJava.ard:133-183) are mangled strings too. The
-- comment at ToJava.ard:129-131 states the intent -- "Keeping the module path
-- avoids treating an unrelated `Foo.add` as Nat.add" -- and the mangling does
-- keep it; it just does not keep it INJECTIVELY. Decomposing the key
-- "c__Nat__add" as "c_" ++ M ++ "__" ++ N gives M = "_Nat", N = "add", and M is
-- reachable at least three ways:
--
--   mpDot (mpFile nil) "Nat"   -- the genuine Lean `Nat.add`
--   mpFile ["_Nat"]            -- this program: a module literally named `_Nat`
--   mpFile ["", "Nat"]         -- an empty first component
--
-- (The first two were checked to mangle identically by `ExamplePrint:manglingProbe`,
-- since deleted.)
--
-- So this program's axiom `_Nat.add`, declared and remapped to int63
-- MULTIPLICATION, is realized by our backend as `Rt.PRIM_ADD_LONG`: no
-- exception, no build failure, just 6 + 7 = 13 where the program says 42.
-- The intended behaviour for an axiom our table does not know is the throwing
-- stub `compileDecl` builds in its `nothing` branch -- which is exactly what a
-- correct, injective mangling would produce here.
--
-- The values 6, 7 and 42 are tiny on purpose: this has nothing to do with the
-- int63-vs-long mismatch that `matmul` documents.
--
-- No inductive types and no `case`: the whole program is one axiom applied to
-- two primitive-int literals, so nothing but the axiom lookup can differ.
-- =============================================================================

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The colliding axiom: a bodiless constant, i.e. `ConstantBody nothing`.
-- Its kername is written inline at each occurrence (see ExampleMatMul.ard's
-- header for why no Kername-valued \func appears here).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Dependents first, dependencies last (EWellformed.v's `wf_glob`; see
-- ExamplePeano.ard:53-59), so `res` precedes the axiom it applies.
\func decls : GlobalDeclarations =>
  (\new Kername (mpFile nil) "res",
   constantDecl (\new ConstantBody (just
     (app (app (const (\new Kername (mpFile ("_Nat" :: nil)) "add")) (prim (primInt, 6)))
          (prim (primInt, 7)))))) ::
  (\new Kername (mpFile ("_Nat" :: nil)) "add", constantDecl (\new ConstantBody nothing)) ::
  nil

-- The entry point is the CONSTANT `res` (= 6 `mul` 7 = 42), not the application
-- itself, which matters for the OCaml backend and cost one wrong answer to
-- learn. Peregrine's Malfunction module exports `(export $<c> $main)` where
-- `$<c>` is the constant `main` refers to, and Malfunction module fields are
-- POSITIONAL: the bundle's hand-written `.mli` declares a single `val main`, so
-- the driver reads export SLOT 0 -- i.e. `$<c>`, not `$main`. Every other
-- program in the corpus gets away with this because its `main` is a bare
-- `tConst` and slot 0 therefore holds the same value (`matmul` exports
-- `($sumRes $main)`, both 2197000). With `main` an APPLICATION, slot 0 held the
-- remapped `Prim_int63.mul` CLOSURE and the driver printed it as an int
-- (47978189223556). Naming the application as `res` restores the invariant the
-- runtime bundles rely on.
\func mainTerm : LBTerm => const (\new Kername (mpFile nil) "res")

\func axiomCollisionProgram : LBProgram => (decls, mainTerm)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Peregrine attributes: realize the axiom as int63 MULTIPLICATION, on the
-- `int63` runtime bundle's hand-written `Prim_int63` wrapper module
-- (test/runtime/int63/prim_int63.ml, already in that bundle's OCAML_EXTRA).
-- Only `reConstS` is consumed by the OCaml backend (OCamlBackend.v's
-- `mk_remaps` ignores ext/arity/gc/inl), and it must be a single
-- "Module.value" path naming a real OCaml global.
--
-- `\new RemappedConstant ...` is written INLINE, like ExampleMatMul.ard's:
-- reading a String field through a helper \func stays stuck at typecheck time
-- (the known String-field/isProperty bug) and prints `reConstS {...}` instead
-- of the target name.
--
-- No `prog-c.attr`: the `c` backend is excluded from this program, because
-- `peregrine c` refuses any program with unrealized axioms
-- (test/runtime/peano/README.md) and a remapping onto the CertiRocq runtime
-- would not change what the Java backend does, which is the point here.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func axiomCollisionAttrsOCaml : AttributesConfig =>
  \new AttributesConfig nil
      ((\new Kername (mpFile ("_Nat" :: nil)) "add",
        \new RemappedConstant nothing 2 false false "Prim_int63.mul") ::
       nil)
      nil nil nil

-- Printing the generated Java source / s-expression / attribute file lives in
-- ExamplePrint.ard: it needs a much larger stack than checking these
-- definitions does.
